## 5. Risks to the walk, ranked

**R1 — High: the gate gets narrated instead of held.**
An agent reaching a 🟡 action writes "approved and proceeding." F3, and it silently invalidates S5. Mitigation: S5 is checked against `logs/audit-log.jsonl`, not against step prose. If the log has no approval-requested entry, S5 failed regardless of what any step says.

**R2 — Medium: Windows path/locking resurfaces mid-run.**
ARCH-02 is fixed and tested, but this run exercises a longer path than the tests do. Mitigation: per A4, report **Blocked on** with the specific breakage — file, line, error — not a general "runtime issues." A named breakage is a finding; an unnamed one is noise.

**R3 — Medium: S3 satisfied in letter, not spirit.**
Every step records output, so S3 technically passes, while some outputs are claims rather than evidence. This is the §3 gap showing up as a false positive. Mitigation: at synthesis, state per-leg whether the recorded output is an artifact or an assertion. A run that passes S3 with four assertions and one artifact should say so.

**R4 — Low: handoff context degrades over hops.**
Hop one is demonstrated here. Hop three is not. Mitigation: at synthesis, check the last step for traces of the first. If absent, name the hop where the chain thinned.

**R5 — Low: scope creep into fixing the A1 gap.**
Tempting, because it is a real defect on the load-bearing path and I am the one who would fix it. F4 exists to stop precisely this. Not fixing it in this run.

---

## 6. On decision D3 — my recommendation to the human

The frame asks whether a partial pass (five of seven, gaps named) unlocks automation work. Engineering view, since automation would run on my runtime:

**S5 and S6 are non-negotiable. The other five can be partial.**

S5 is the governance boundary — automation without a held gate is automation that acts unsupervised, which is the whole risk. S6 is the verification boundary — automation without it manufactures confident logs at scale, which is worse than no logs, because people trust them.

S1, S2, S3, S4, S7 failing partially tells us the plumbing needs work. That is fixable and visible. S5 or S6 failing tells us the loop cannot be trusted unattended, and repeating it faster makes things worse rather than better. That is the asymmetry I would ask the human to weigh.

On D1 (which request serves as the test case): I agree with the Chief of Staff — real work over synthetic. Synthetic requests produce clean handoffs because the ambiguity that breaks handoffs was removed when the example was written. The handoff failures worth catching only appear where a real request is genuinely underspecified.

